{
  "requirement": "When implementation shows that agreed requirement text must change, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to stop work that depends on the change and take the changed requirements through review, agreement, and commit before continuing.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The text of `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.text_change` is: \"the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to stop work that depends on the change and take the changed requirements through review, agreement, and commit before continuing.\" It names no library, algorithm, signature, type, field, or delegation through a named interface. It says only what the skill's guidance must tell agents to do.\n\nI tested the candidate clause \"the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents\". An alternative that put the same guidance in a different section of `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md` would behave the same and still satisfy the text. So where the guidance sits in the file is discretionary, and the file's section layout is not a structural obligation.\n\nThe review root is `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`. Its outline has `Workflow`, `Writing chapters`, `Choosing references`, `What the review checks` and `Gotchas` sections. The tool output elided the paragraph bodies, so I did not confirm the wording itself. That wording is the observable behavior the requirement states. It is not a structural obligation that needs implementation evidence.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement has one behavioral obligation. When implementation shows agreed text must change, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to stop dependent work. It must also direct them to take the changed requirements through review, agreement and commit before continuing.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_stops_dependent_work_when_agreed_text_must_change]` covers it in two parts:\n- **Directive:** `assert_phrases` runs `assert_text` over `bundled_skill(\"habeas-requirements\")`, which is `SKILL.md` plus its reference files. It asserts the skill contains \"If implementation shows that agreed text must change, stop work that depends on it and take the changed requirements through steps 3 to 5 first\". That sentence has the trigger, the stop-dependent-work instruction and the route through review before continuing. The phrase is present in `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, in the Workflow section paragraph.\n- **Steps 3 to 5:** the test calls `step(\"habeas-requirements\", n)`, built on `workflow_steps`, and asserts that step 3 starts with \"Review.\", step 4 with \"Present\" and step 5 with \"Commit.\". This checks that the referenced steps are review, agreement (presenting to the user) and commit. A reworded or renumbered workflow would therefore fail the test.\n\nI found no distinct modes, boundaries or failure cases the text names. The helpers `assert_phrases`, `assert_text`, `step`, `workflow_steps` and `bundled_skill` assert for real, since a missing phrase or step panics. The test is marked `{test}` for the subject, so there is no marking gap.",
